nActually, a subtlety: he is stuck against the pipe at x=210 with camera such that he's at screen x ~112. Backing up 24 frames at 1.5px/frame = 36 px. Then he needs ~40px of runway to reach full run speed (accel). Total gap to pipe ~36px. Hmm, he'd reach maybe 1.6 px/frame at the pipe. A running jump at full speed gives more height? In SMB, jump height doesn't depend on speed much (the PlayerYSpdData uses speed class: Y=0 standing gives -4, Y=3-4 run gives -5). So a running jump rises higher (initial speed -5 vs -4). That might make the difference.\n\nMaximum jump height with -4 initial and gravity... from the mapping: standing jump apex moved 176->124 = 52 px. With running (-5 initial), maybe ~65 px. That's exactly the 4-block pipe (64px). Tight.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually in the real SMB 1-1, the tall pipes (4 tiles) — Mario jumps over them regularly. He can do it from a run.\n\nAlternative: bounce off the pipe: jump and land on top? Can't reach.\n\nAlternative: avoid this pipe entirely? No.\n\nAlternative clever approach: since jump height is limited, use the star? no.\n\nHmm, another thought: maybe I misidentified and it's the pipe at x=736 which is 4 tiles high, and Mario's stuck because he's *under* a lower ceiling? No.\n\nLet me first verify the situation with get_tile_at_pixel / read_nametable to know the pipe height and Mario's y. Actually simpler: let me watch $00CE (y) and try the back-up run.\n\nLet me do the back-up-run approach.\n\nScript: [{left,30},{right,b,30},{right,b,a,22},{right,b,30}] w/ watch 134,206,1820,134.\n\nTotal 112 frames. Refresh star first.\n\nActually careful: pressing left at the pipe, Mario will walk left (no pit behind presumably).\n\nLet's go."}
