thing else, and both Goombas and Koopas have... no.\n\nLet me look at the 1-1 enemy list from ROM: I could decode it to find the types. Let me try again with the actual SMB format.\n\nI'm now fairly confident about the real SMB enemy data format (from smbdis):\n```\n;enemy data: 2 bytes per enemy\n;  byte 0: x (in tiles, 16px units)... \n```\nHmm, but our parse as 3-byte records worked nicely with the $0F rule. Let me examine the resulting records:\n(1E,C2,00), (6B,06,8B), (86,63,B7), PAGE(0F,05), (03,06,23), (06,4B,B7), (BB,00,5B), (B7,FB,37), (3B,B7,0F), (0B,1B,37)\n\nHmm, byte0 values: 1E, 6B, 86, 0F, 03, 06, BB, B7, 3B, 0B. \n\nHmm, in the real format I believe byte0 is the X in *pages*: bits 7-4 = page number (in 256px units... no).\n\nActually here's the real SMB enemy data format (I'm now recalling the disassembly comments):\n\n```\n;Enemy data format:\n;  Each enemy has 2 bytes:\n;    byte 0: X coordinate in \"screens\"? \n;  Actually: the first byte is the X position where the high nibble is the page (0-F, 256px each) and the low nibble is the x within the page in 16px units (0-15 => 0-240px).\n;    byte 1: bits 7-6 = y? bits 5-0 = enemy type?\n```\n\nTest: entry (1E,C2): byte0=$1E → page 1 (256px), x within = 14*16=224 → x = 256+224 = 480. Hmm, but the first Goomba in 1-1 is at ~352. Hmm, close-ish but no.\n\nAlternatively byte0 = page 1, x = 224 → 224? no.\n\nHmm, with 3-byte records and byte2 as the type: types = 00, 8B, B7, 23, B7, 5B, 37, 0F, 37. Those don't look like small type IDs.\n\nSo maybe byte0 = x within page (in tiles?), byte1 = ?, byte2 = ? Let me think about positions: known Goomba positions in 1-1 (from our observations): \n- Goomba near x=352? (killed early)\n- Goombas at ~1395/1533/1557 (we saw them around x=1475-1557)\n- Goombas at ~1810+\nHmm, and we saw at x≈2110 a Goomba behind at x≈2100.\n\nThe entries with $B7 as byte0: (B7,FB,37) and (B7,00,5B)... and (3B,B7,0F). Hmm, $B7 = 183.\n\nHmm what if byte0 is x/16 (tile units) and the pair is (x_tile, y/type)? 183*16 = 2928. Hmm.\n\nWhat if the records are 3 bytes: [x_tile, y_tile_and_flags, type]? \n(1E,C2,00): x_tile=0x1E=30 → 480px. Hmm.\n\nHmm, what about the \"page\" interpretation with the low nibble $F marker: The mapping says 2-byte entries when the low nibble of byte0 is $0F. The entry (0F,05) is a \"page entry\" — maybe it means \"advance to the next page\"? The value $05 could be a page number? Hmm: it appears at index 9, i.e. after 3 records.\n\nHonestly, without the disassembly this is a rabbit hole. Let me abandon decoding and just play.\n\nActually — alternative: use lookup_symbol! The task says \"No reference is available.\" So no.\n\nOK. Continue playing. Jump the pipe.\n\nNote: I should save a checkpoint here (x=2594, blocked by a pipe, on the ground). Let me save slot 5 after jumping.\n\nLet me jump the pipe now."}
