at about the screenshot at x≈2850 (the pipe + final staircase)? There was a Goomba behind at (30,205).\n\nHmm, no Koopa seen.\n\nWait a moment! Maybe the Koopa Troopa in 1-1 IS at the beginning-ish and I saw it early: the \"green blob\" I dismissed as a bush at x≈453 (the screenshot at x=395 with the green thing next to the pipe, which I thought might be a Koopa). Hmm! Let me re-examine that screenshot: it showed a green blob at (115,205) — I decided it was a bush.\n\nHmm, and the task says \"the first one in 1-1 is far to the right, past the pipes\" — so the Koopa is past the 4 pipes. Our first \"past the pipes\" area is x≈1000+.\n\nHmm, we passed x=1000-3000 without a Koopa ID.\n\nHmm, unless the Koopa's ID is... let me check what ID a Koopa would have. If the Koopa's ID happens to be 6 (same as Goomba)?? Hmm — wait! What if $0016 isn't the ID but the *enemy's y-velocity or direction*, and both Goombas and Koopas have 6?\n\nHmm! Let's test: the flagpole object's ID=48 ($30) is in $001B. If $0016 were a velocity, the flagpole object having 48 there would be odd. So $0016 is likely an ID/type byte.\n\nSo Goomba ID = 6. Koopa ID = ? Probably 0? or 1?\n\nHmm, if the Koopa's ID is 0, my watch (which showed 0 for empty slots) would NOT distinguish it. But I'd see its flag ($000F) = 1 with ID 0.\n\nIn the stretch at x=1810-2110, flags: slot0=1 ID=6, slot2=1 ID=6. In the stretch 2594-2850: slot1 (address 22 = $0016) = 0 with flags... I watched flags in some stretches.\n\nHmm, in the stretch ending at x=2594, the IDs were all 0 and flags weren't watched, but earlier stretches showed flags 1 with IDs 6.\n\nUgh.\n\nOK, let me take a completely different approach to find the Koopa: let me read the 1-1 enemy data from ROM and decode it properly using actual SMB knowledge. Let me look up the SMB enemy data format online? I have web search! The task says \"No reference is available\" (meaning no imported symbol reference), but I can search the web for the SMB 1-1 enemy list.\n\n