29,tile_y=18) is 163 ($A3).\n\nSo the discrepancy suggests read_nametable and get_tile_at_pixel disagree — possibly because the scroll split means the pole is drawn from nametable 1 (the second nametable) or the read is of the wrong nametable. get_tile_at_pixel says nametable 0, nametable_addr=8781 = 0x224D, which is nametable 0 ($2000), row 18, col 13.\n\nHmm, 8781-8192 = 589; 589 = 18*32 + 13. So col 13, row 18. But the tool says tile_x=29, tile_y=18.\n\nHmm, so tile_x=29 but the address col is 13? That's inconsistent unless there's a horizontal scroll offset. With scroll_x=128, the screen x=232 → the tile column in the nametable = (232 + scroll_x)/8 = 360/8 = 45 → mod 32 = 13. Ah, so the nametable column is 13, and tile_x=29 is the *screen* tile column (232/8 = 29). So get_tile_at_pixel's tile_x is the screen tile column, and the nametable address is the correct one.\n\nBut my read_nametable(28,3) read *nametable* coordinates, i.e. columns 28-31 of the nametable, which is a different part of the level. That's why it showed blanks. I should read at column 13, rows 3-20.\n\nOK, so let me redo: read_nametable(x=13, y=4, width=2, height=16) to see the pole's tiles.\n\nAlso the ball: get_region at the pole top. Let me get_tile_at_pixel at (232,50) (near the ball) and (232,60).\n\nLet me also determine the pole's full extent and the ball tile.\n\nThen the flag: get_region object mode at (228,52) — the white flag.\n\nLet me batch:\n- read_nametable(13, 4, 3, 16)\n- get_tile_at_pixel(232,50)\n- get_region object mode (228,52)\n- get_pixel_color(232,46) (ball)\n\nLet me do these."}
