e screenshot looks like a small green circle at the top of the pole, about 8x8. Let me check the pixel at (232,44): get_pixel_color.\n\nHmm, let me get the ball's tiles properly: read_tile $12F and $13D, and get_region object mode at (232,44).\n\nAlso, the flag: get_region object mode at (228,52) → sprite layer, palette 5, slots 1,2, 136 pixels, bbox (216,49)-(231,64) = 16x16, CHR tiles $7E (126) and $7F (127), OAM #28,#29,#30.\n\nSo the flag is a 16x16 sprite metatile using CHR $7E and $7F, sprite palette 5.\n\nHmm, but the OAM entries listed: (216,49) tile $7E, (224,49) tile $7F, (224,57) tile $7E. That's 3 entries: $7E at top-left, $7F at top-right, $7E at bottom-right. Hmm, so the sprite is made of: $7E (upper-left), $7F (upper-right), $7E (lower-right) — and the lower-left? Maybe the flag's left part is shorter.\n\nActually, the flag is a triangle: the top-left tile $7E, top-right $7F, bottom-right $7E (the triangle's lower part). Hmm.\n\nWait, the visible flag in the screenshot is a small white triangle pointing left, about 8x8? Hmm. Whatever — the data is what matters.\n\nHmm, one issue: the flag's OAM entries: #28 (216,49) $7E, #29 (224,49) $7F, #30 (224,57) $7E. Hmm, #30 is at y=57 which is 8 below #29. So entries: top row: $7E at 216, $7F at 224; bottom row: $7E at 224 only. So a 16x16 box with the lower-left missing → the flag shape (a pennant).\n\nNow, note this is the flag's state at the pole top (not yet sliding). \n\nAlso, the enemy ID 48 at slot 5 = the flagpole object.\n\nNow — the Koopa! Hmm, we still need it.\n\nWait... maybe the \"first Koopa Troopa in 1-1\" is actually the enemy I killed early?! Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the 1-1 enemy list once more. Let me actually decode the ROM enemy list using the structure I found (3-byte records):\n(1E,C2,00), (6B,06,8B), (86,63,B7), PAGE(0F,05), (03,06,23), (06,4B,B7), (BB,00,5B), (B7,FB,37), (3B,B7,0F), (0B,1B,37)\n\nHmm, hmm. Actually! I remember the SMB enemy data format now (from smbdis \"AreaEnemyData\"):\n\n```\n;Enemy data format:\n;  b0: x position (in \"16-pixel\" units? actually the high nibble is the page and the low nibble is the x within the page in 16px units)\n;  b1: y position (high nibble?) and ...\n```\n\nHmm, alternatively the real format:\nEach enemy is 2 bytes:\n- byte 0: X coordinate (in 16px units, 0-255 → up to 4080px)\n- byte 1: bits 7-4 = Y coordinate? bits 3-0 = enemy type?\n\nTest: (1E,C2) → x = 0x1E*16 = 480. type = $2? Hmm.\n(00,6B) → x=0, type=$B?\nHmm no.\n\nLet me try: byte1 high nibble = enemy type? (1E,C2): type = $C? no.\n\nOK here's the thing — I recall the SMB enemy data format more precisely now:\n\n```\n;Enemy data:\n;  Each entry is 2 bytes:\n;    byte 0: X coordinate in tiles (16px), where the value is a multiple of... \n;    byte 1: bits: yyyy tttt  (y = y offset? t = type)\n```\nHmm.\n\nLet me test with a known: the flagpole object. Its Enemy_ID was 48 = $30. If the data's type field for the flagpole is $30, look for $30 in the data: the bytes are 1E C2 00 6B 06 8B 86 63 B7 0F 05 03 06 23 06 4B B7 BB 00 5B B7 FB 37 3B B7 0F 0B 1B 37 FF. Hmm, no $30. Hmm! So the flagpole isn't from the enemy list (the enemy list has no $30). \n\nHmm, but the flagpole object DID appear in the enemy array ($0016 slot 5 = 48 = $30)! So the flagpole is spawned as an enemy object from... the *layout* (object) list! The layout data at $A690 has 2-byte records; one of them is the flagpole object. And the object spawner creates an enemy slot for it? Hmm.\n\nHmm, interesting: so the \"flagpole\" object in the layout list spawns into the enemy array with ID $30 — actually wait. Hmm, in SMB the flagpole is indeed an object in the *level layout* (the same list that places blocks), with object type $30? Hmm, no.\n\nHmm, whatever. The important insight: the enemy array's ID 48 came from the flagpole, not from the enemy list.\n\nSo, back to the Koopa: it must be in the enemy list. Let's decode: The enemy list has 10 records (3-byte) as parsed. The value $30 doesn't appear, so the Koopa's type must be one of: 00, 8B, B7, 23, 5B, 37, 0F, 37 (byte2 values) or from byte1.\n\nHmm, those look like *positions*, not types. E.g., $B7 repeated. Hmm! $0F appears as a \"page marker\" byte0 with byte1=$05.\n\nNew idea: maybe the format is: byte0 = x, byte1 = y_and_flags, byte2 = ??? Hmm.\n\nLet me try assuming the enemy \"type\" is byte1's low nibble or byte2's low nibble and see if the number of Goombas/Koopas matches the level:\nbyte2 low nibbles: 0, B, 7, 3, 7, B, 7, F, 7.\nbyte1 low nibbles: 2, 6, 3, 5, 6, B, 0, B, 7, B.\n\nHmm, we observed Goomba ID = 6. So maybe the type is byte1 (values: C2, 06, 63, 05, 06, 4B, 00, FB, B7, 1B)? Hmm, ID 6 vs data value 6: entries with byte1 = $06: (6B,06,8B) and (03,06,23). Hmm, and our observed Goombas were at x≈480?, 1542?, 2144?... \n\nHmm, wait: we observed Goombas at ~1395?, 1533, 1557, 2100, 2500, 2762.\n\nHmm, entry (6B,06,8B): if x comes from byte0's high nibble = 6 → page 6 = 1536, plus... the low nibble B*16 = 176 → 1536+176 = 1712? Hmm, or x = byte0*16 = 6B*16 = 1712. Hmm.\n\nHmm, the two Goombas we saw at 1533 and 1557 are ~24px apart — a Goomba pair. In SMB, a \"Goomba pair\" is spawned by a single data entry? Hmm, no, there are separate entries (or the spawner spawns a pair for certain types).\n\nHmm, honestly, let me try the web. A quick web search for \"Super Mario Bros 1-1 enemy data format\" or the 1-1 enemy list would settle where the Koopa is. Let me do that — it's fast and authoritative.\n\nActually, better: let me just RE-PLAY the region 936-1475 without the star and watch IDs. That's empirical and certain. It's ~3 stretches.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the Koopa might have been killed by the star in that region, and its ID would have shown during my stretches... which I didn't watch. So replaying with IDs watched will reveal it.\n\nLet me do that. But first, let me finish the flagpole mapping while we're here.\n\nLet me gather:\n1. The pole ball tiles: read_