{"type":"thinking","text":"The web search gave a promising source: patpend.net \"smbenemy.txt\" — an enemy data disassembly. Let me fetch it! That should tell me the enemy list format and possibly the 1-1 Koopa location.\n\nMeanwhile, the play: Mario x went 20→195 (page 5 → 1280+195 = 1475) — wait, \"first 20, last 195\". Hmm, starting page: the previous stretch ended at 1295. So x=20 → 1300 (p5). Ends at 195 → 1475. y=67 at the end (mid-jump). \n\nFlags: all active enemies had ID 6 (Goombas). No Koopa.\n\nSo in the range 936→1475 (with the star off this time), no Koopa spawned. Hmm!\n\nSo where's the Koopa? Maybe further along: 1475-2110 (where I previously saw only ID 6)? I watched IDs there in the earlier run — all 6. Hmm, but those were with the star on... but IDs don't care.\n\nHmm, so no Koopa anywhere from 936 to the staircase?! \n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the Koopa's ID is 6! Let me verify: is ID 6 the Goomba or could it be something else? We saw ID 6 for enemies drawn with tiles $70-$73 (Goomba). Definitely Goomba.\n\nHmm, so the Koopa's ID is something else. And I haven't seen any other ID except 48 (the flagpole object) at the very end.\n\nWait... unless the Koopa IS one of the objects with ID 6?? No.\n\nHmm, hold on! Maybe the \"first Koopa Troopa in 1-1\" is *before* the pipes... no, the user said past the pipes.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe the enemy data type for the Koopa maps to ID 6 too?! I.e., $0016 might be the *Enemy_Type* (a group), and both Goombas and Koopas could be... no, they must differ (they behave differently).\n\nHmm, wait! Actually — hold on! What if $0016 is not the enemy ID but the enemy's *y-speed* or *\"Enemy_Move\"*? Hmm. If $0016 were a movement byte, then a Koopa and a Goomba could both have 6.\n\nHmm, let's test: the flagpole object had $001B = 48. If $001B were a movement byte for the flagpole object, 48 = $30 makes no sense as a speed. So $0016-$001B is more likely an ID... but hmm, actually $30 could be a \"direction/velocity\" for the flagpole? Unlikely.\n\nHmm, but here's the thing: in SMB, is Enemy_ID at $0016? Let me check with the SMB RAM map: \n- $000F: Enemy_Flag (6 bytes)\n- $0016: Enemy_ID (6 bytes)?? Hmm, hmm. Actually I recall:\n  - $000F Enemy_Flag\n  - $0016 Enemy_ID\n  - $001E Enemy_State\nHmm, that matches the mapping's note (\"$0016\" mentioned as the same object-slot indexing). And Enemy_ID for a Goomba in SMB is... \n\nI'm now fairly convinced: in SMB, the enemy IDs are:\n$00 = Goomba? Hmm, but we see 6.\n\nHmm! Actually, hold on. I remember the SMB enemy ID table:\n```\nEnemy_ID:\n$00 = Goomba (Little Goomba)\n$01 = ... \n```\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, from the smbdis \"Enemy_ID\" constants:\n```\nEnemy_ID_Goomba = $06? \n```\nHmm, hmm. You know, I DO now recall that in SMB, the Enemy_ID for a Goomba is $06? Hmm, no...\n\nActually! I remember the enemy data format: the level enemy data's type byte uses values like:\n$00 = Goomba, $01 = ... no.\n\nLet me just fetch the patpend.net smbenemy.txt — it's the enemy data documentation. That should settle it.\n\nActually, better: let me fetch the smbdis PDF? No, let me fetch smbenemy.txt first (small text file).\n\nLet me also think: maybe in 1-1 there are NO Koopas at all?! Hmm! Wait — is that possible? Let me think about World 1-1 in SMB... \n\nHmm, actually, hmm! I'm now doubting myself. Let me recall playing 1-1: after the pipes... there's the pit, then the brick platforms with Goombas, then the double staircase, then the final staircase, then the flagpole. Koopas... hmm. I'm fairly sure there's a Koopa Troopa in 1-1 — the \"first Koopa Troopa\" is iconic. Hmm, but where?\n\nOH WAIT. I remember now!! In World 1-1, the Koopa Troopa is on the ground right before the *first pit*? No...\n\nHmm, let me just fetch the enemy data doc. That will give me the list and the type values, and I can decode the 1-1 enemy list myself.\n\nLet me fetch patpend.net/technical/nes/smbenemy.txt."}
