{"type":"thinking","text":"Now I have the enemy data format:\n- Enemy data starts at ROM 0x1F04 (without header). In our ROM, the mapping says the enemy list is at prgRom 0x1F01. Hmm, the doc says 1F04 (for the untrained rom). Our mapping says the list starts at prgRom 0x1F01 (which starts with $1E — the mapping says \"the list begins byte0=$1E\"). Hmm, the doc says the data is 2 bytes per enemy: byte0 = x (upper nibble = x, lower nibble = y), byte1 = enemy type.\n\nSo with our data at 0x1F01: [1E, C2, 00, 6B, 06, 8B, 86, 63, B7, 0F, 05, 03, 06, 23, 06, 4B, B7, BB, 00, 5B, B7, FB, 37, 3B, B7, 0F, 0B, 1B, 37, FF]\n\nIf the data starts at 0x1F01: pairs (1E,C2), (00,6B), (06,8B), (86,63), (B7,0F), (05,03), (06,23), (06,4B), (B7,BB), (00,5B), (B7,FB), (37,3B), (B7,0F), (0B,1B), (37,FF).\n\nHmm, that doesn't look right. But if the data starts at 0x1F04 (as the doc says): bytes from index 3: [6B, 06, 8B, 86, 63, B7, 0F, 05, 03, 06, 23, 06, 4B, B7, BB, 00, 5B, B7, FB, 37, 3B, B7, 0F, 0B, 1B, 37, FF]\n\nPairs: (6B,06), (8B,86), (63,B7), (0F,05), (03,06), (23,06), (4B,B7), (BB,00), (5B,B7), (FB,37), (3B,B7), (0F,0B), (1B,37), (FF,?) → hmm, the terminator is byte0 = $FF.\n\nInteresting! With the doc's format: byte0 = x,y nibbles, byte1 = enemy type:\n(6B, 06): x = 6, y = B, type = 06 = GOOMBA ✓ (the doc says \"the first byte for the first goomba is 6B\"!) — CONFIRMED! The doc explicitly says the first goomba's first byte is 6B. And our data at 0x1F04 begins with 6B! \n\nSo the enemy list is at 0x1F04, and it's 2-byte records: (x_nibble, y_nibble), type.\n\nWait, but then where does the x page come from? The doc says \"the upper 4 bits represent the x value\" — but 6 is only 0-15. Hmm, the doc says changing 6B to 0B makes the goomba start closer to the level start. So the upper nibble is a *page* (each page = 256 px). So x = 6*256 + ...? Hmm, and what about within-page x? The \"y value\" lower nibble is the y? Hmm, that doesn't give within-page x.\n\nHmm, in the real SMB enemy data format, byte0: bits 7-4 = page (x/256), bits 3-0 = y (in tiles? 0-15). Hmm, so the x within the page is always... hmm, that gives x = page*256 + 0? That's too coarse (enemies would only be at page boundaries).\n\nHmm, but actually, I recall the real format: the enemy data byte0's high nibble is the page AND the low nibble is the y; the x within the page comes from... hmm. Hmm, wait — in the actual SMB the enemy list format is:\n\nbyte0: x position: the high nibble is the page (256px), the low nibble is the y position (tile rows?).\nbyte1: bits 7-4 = x within the page? and bits 3-0 = enemy type!\n\nHmm! That makes sense: byte1 = (x_offset_in_tiles << 4) | type? Let me test with the first pair: (6B, 06): byte1 = $06 → x within page = 0, type = 6 = Goomba ✓. x = 6*256 + 0 = 1536? Hmm, but the first Goomba in 1-1 is at ~350.\n\nHmm, that doesn't work. Hmm, but the doc says \"Changing it to 0B makes the goomba start closer to the start of the level\" — so 6B → 0B moves it closer, meaning the HIGH nibble is the coarse x. With 6 → x≈1536? That's not the first Goomba.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the high nibble is not a page but a \"x / 16 tiles\"? Hmm.\n\nHmm, hold on. The doc also says \"There are two bytes which show where an enemy is and what it is. The first byte appears to be where the enemy is located, and the second one shows what the enemy is.\"\n\nSo byte0 = position (x high nibble, y low nibble), byte1 = enemy type ONLY. Then the x within the page must come from... hmm, maybe each entry only specifies the page and the exact position is fixed? No.\n\nHmm! Actually, I remember now: in SMB the enemy x position is 4 bits of page + 4 bits of y, but the *page* here is 16 tiles = 256 px... and the x within the page is always tile 0? Hmm, but enemies can be at any x.\n\nHmm, hmm. Actually — maybe the format is: byte0 = x (in tiles, 0-255 = 0-4080px)? No: 6B = 107 tiles = 1712px. And the doc says changing 6 to 0 moves it closer. If byte0 were a plain x/16, then 6B = 107*16 = 1712. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait: maybe byte0 = (page << 4) | y, where the page is 256px, and byte1's high nibble = the x within the page in 16px units. Then (6B,06): x = 6*256 + 0*16 = 1536, y = 11. Hmm, but the first Goomba in 1-1 is at ~350 (≈ page 1, tile 6).\n\nHmm! Unless the first Goomba entry isn't the first record. The doc says the data starts at 1F04 and the first goomba is 6B. In our ROM, is there data before 0x1F04? Our list began at 0x1F01 with [1E, C2, 00]. Hmm, so 0x1F01 = 1E, 0x1F02 = C2, 0x1F03 = 00, 0x1F04 = 6B.\n\nHmm, so per the doc, the enemy data starts at 0x1F04 = 6B. So the first record is (6B, 06) = a Goomba. Hmm, but a Goomba at x=1536 as the first enemy? That can't be right for 1-1.\n\nHmm, unless the ROM has a header offset. The doc says \"in the untrained rom without a header\". Our mapping's prgRom offsets are also without the iNES header. And our read at 0x1F01 gave [1E,C2,00,6B,06,...]. The doc says the first goomba's byte is 6B, and indeed 6B is at 0x1F04.\n\nHmm, so maybe our list starts at 0x1F04 and the bytes 1E C2 00 before it are the pointer/header. Hmm, the mapping said the list starts at $9F01 with 30 bytes. Hmm.\n\nHmm, but 0x1F01... maybe the mapping is off by 3.\n\nOK, so let's decode from 0x1F04 with 2-byte records:\n(6B,06): Goomba\n(8B,86): hmm, byte1 = $86 → type $06 = Goomba? with the high nibble 8 = x within page = 8*16 = 128? So x = 11*256 + 128 = 2944?? Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the format is:\nbyte0: x high nibble = page, low nibble = y\nbyte1: high nibble = x within the page (in 16px units), low nibble = type\n\n(6B,06): page 6, x=0 → 1536, y=11, Goomba.\nHmm, but hold on — maybe the page numbering here isn't 256px. Since the y nibble takes the low nibble, we only get x = page*256. And the high nibble of byte1 gives the within-page x in 16px units → 0-240. That gives full x resolution! So x = page*256 + (byte1>>4)*16.\n\n(6B,06): x = 1536 + 0 = 1536, type 6 (Goomba) ✓ — matches our observed Goombas at ~1533!! And the doc's \"first goomba is 6B\" — hmm, but the first Goomba we saw was at ~352...\n\nHmm, wait: maybe the doc's example is just about the first record in the list, and in 1-1 the first *data* record might correspond to an enemy later in the level? Hmm, no — the spawner processes them in order as Mario advances, so the first record should be the earliest enemy.\n\nHmm, but our observations: the first Goomba (x≈352) — I killed it with the star early. Then Goombas at 1533/1557.\n\nHmm, so with the decoding: \nrecords from 0x1F04 (2 bytes each):\n1. (6B, 06): x = 6*256 + (0*16) = 1536, y=11, Goomba\n2. (8B, 86): x = 8*256 + 128 = 2176, type 6 = Goomba\n3. (63, B7): x = 6*256 + (B*16) = 1536+176 = 1712, type 7 = BLOOPER?? Hmm.\n4. (0F, 05): x = 0*256 + 0 = 0, type 5 = Hammer Brother?? Hmm.\n5. (03, 06): x = 0 + 0 = 0, type 6 Goomba\n6. (23, 06): x = 2*256 + 0 = 512, type 6\n7. (4B, B7): x = 4*256+176 = 1200, type 7 = Blooper\n8. (BB, 00): x = 11*256 = 2816, type 0 = Green Koopa!!\n9. (5B, B7): x = 5*256+176 = 1456, type 7 = Blooper\n10. (FB, 37): x = 15*256+48 = 3888, type 7? hmm\n...\n\nThis is inconsistent (Bloopers in 1-1?? no). So my byte-pairing must be wrong.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider: maybe the terminator byte0 = $FF ends the list, and the entries before it: from 0x1F04 to the $FF at 0x1F1E: bytes 0x1F04..0x1F1D = 26 bytes = 13 records:\n6B 06 | 8B 86 | 63 B7 | 0F 05 | 03 06 | 23 06 | 4B B7 | BB 00 | 5B B7 | FB 37 | 3B B7 | 0F 0B | 1B 37 | FF\n\nHmm, 13 records + terminator. Hmm, the types (byte1's low nibble): 6, 6, 7, 5, 6, 6, 7, 0, 7, 7, 7, B, 7. Hmm: 6=Goomba, 0=Green Koopa, 7=Blooper, 5=Hammer Brother, B=Red Cheep Cheep. That's wrong for 1-1.\n\nSo the pairing is off. Let me try the 3-byte hypothesis again with the doc's insight: \nOur data from 0x1F01: 1E C2 00 | 6B 06 8B | 86 63 B7 | 0F 05 | 03 06 23 | 06 4B B7 | BB 00 5B | B7 FB 37 | 3B B7 0F | 0B 1B 37 | FF\n\nHmm, with the doc: the first goomba's first byte is 6B. In the 3-byte parse, 6B is the first byte of record 2. So maybe the record format is 3 bytes: [x_page:y_nibbles, x_within_page?, enemy_type]? Hmm, but then byte1 = \"flags/within-page\" per the mapping.\n\nRecord 2: (6B, 06, 8B): byte0 = 6B = page 6, y=11; byte1 = 06; byte2 = 8B?? Hmm, that'd be the type 8B — ","truncated":true}
